WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.941
Cost and operations trade-offs.

2
00:00:02.221 --> 00:00:04.867
The costs that matter are rarely the vector math.

3
00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:13.424
Embedding cost is paid at ingestion and again on every re-embed (a model change, a chunking change, contextual retrieval).

4
00:00:13.704 --> 00:00:21.597
Estimate it as total tokens times the embedding price, and keep the content hash so unchanged documents are never re-embedded.

5
00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:28.021
Storage and memory scale with chunks times dimensions; smaller chunks and overlap multiply both.

6
00:00:28.301 --> 00:00:40.717
HNSW indexes add graph memory and take time to build; pgvector notes HNSW has a better speed-recall trade-off than IVFFlat but slower builds and more memory.

7
00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:43.493
Operations is where options differ most.

8
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:48.147
An embedded store has no server to run but one process owns the files.

9
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:56.064
pgvector inherits everything your team already does for Postgres: backups, monitoring, migrations, access control.

10
00:00:56.344 --> 00:01:01.869
A dedicated database is one more service to secure, upgrade, and page someone about.

11
00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:19.408
A managed service removes that work in exchange for usage-based pricing, data leaving your network, and lock-in, which you can reduce by keeping your ingestion pipeline and chunk store portable (the lab’s JSON file is a crude version of this: you can rebuild any index from it).

12
00:01:19.688 --> 00:01:35.773
A defensible decision for most teams: NumPy or an embedded store while the corpus is small and you are still changing chunking; pgvector if you already run Postgres; a dedicated or managed vector database when scale, multitenancy features,

13
00:01:36.053 --> 00:01:40.107
or hybrid search at high volume justify running or buying one.

14
00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:51.651
Revisit the choice with numbers (latency at your p95, recall@k with the ANN index versus exact search, monthly cost) rather than with a vendor’s benchmark chart.
